WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.967
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C two, Book three.

2
00:00:05.267 --> 00:00:09.095
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.395 --> 00:00:16.180
Unit twenty-six: Migration Policy. Today you will balance a debate using On the one hand... on the other.

4
00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:19.109
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.067 --> 00:00:23.416
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.250 --> 00:00:25.332
Migration.

7
00:00:26.317 --> 00:00:30.267
Migration means the movement of people from one place to another to live.

8
00:00:30.883 --> 00:00:33.603
Migration has shaped the economy of every region.

9
00:00:34.217 --> 00:00:36.602
Your turn. Say: Migration.

10
00:00:39.467 --> 00:00:40.479
Migration.

11
00:00:41.217 --> 00:00:42.160
Policy.

12
00:00:43.150 --> 00:00:46.218
Policy means a course of action adopted by a government.

13
00:00:46.817 --> 00:00:50.488
Migration policy must balance security and compassion.

14
00:00:51.083 --> 00:00:53.422
Your turn. Say: Policy.

15
00:00:56.267 --> 00:00:57.163
Policy.

16
00:00:57.917 --> 00:00:58.732
Labour.

17
00:00:59.717 --> 00:01:02.308
Labour means work, or workers as a group.

18
00:01:02.917 --> 00:01:05.532
Labour shortages drove demand for migrants.

19
00:01:06.117 --> 00:01:08.409
Your turn. Say: Labour.

20
00:01:11.267 --> 00:01:12.036
Labour.

21
00:01:12.783 --> 00:01:13.831
Refugee.

22
00:01:14.817 --> 00:01:18.627
Refugee means a person forced to flee their country because of danger.

23
00:01:19.217 --> 00:01:21.878
A refugee has the right to seek protection.

24
00:01:22.483 --> 00:01:25.438
Your turn. Say: Refugee.

25
00:01:28.300 --> 00:01:29.382
Refugee.

26
00:01:30.117 --> 00:01:31.222
Integration.

27
00:01:32.217 --> 00:01:36.457
Integration means the process by which newcomers become part of society.

28
00:01:37.050 --> 00:01:40.083
Integration depends on language, work and welcome.

29
00:01:40.683 --> 00:01:43.394
Your turn. Say: Integration.

30
00:01:46.233 --> 00:01:47.200
Integration.

31
00:01:47.950 --> 00:01:49.114
Remittances.

32
00:01:50.117 --> 00:01:53.091
Remittances means money sent home by migrant workers.

33
00:01:53.683 --> 00:01:56.066
Remittances support millions of families.

34
00:01:56.650 --> 00:01:59.744
Your turn. Say: Remittances.

35
00:02:02.600 --> 00:02:03.857
Remittances.

36
00:02:04.617 --> 00:02:05.629
Rights.

37
00:02:06.617 --> 00:02:09.650
Rights means entitlements that others must respect.

38
00:02:10.250 --> 00:02:13.318
Migrant workers must be guaranteed basic rights.

39
00:02:13.917 --> 00:02:16.581
Your turn. Say: Rights.

40
00:02:19.433 --> 00:02:20.481
Rights.

41
00:02:21.217 --> 00:02:22.020
Border.

42
00:02:23.017 --> 00:02:25.724
Border means the line separating two countries.

43
00:02:26.317 --> 00:02:28.154
The border was closed overnight.

44
00:02:28.750 --> 00:02:31.356
Your turn. Say: Border.

45
00:02:34.200 --> 00:02:35.155
Border.

46
00:02:35.967 --> 00:02:39.699
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:40.550 --> 00:02:45.815
Visal, my aunt works abroad and sends money every month. Is migration good or bad?

48
00:02:46.917 --> 00:02:55.258
It depends whom you ask. On the one hand, migration fills labour gaps and remittances support families.

49
00:02:56.350 --> 00:02:57.595
And the other side?

50
00:02:58.683 --> 00:03:07.010
On the other hand, it raises integration challenges and can drain skilled workers from poorer countries.

51
00:03:08.117 --> 00:03:10.023
So what should policy do?

52
00:03:11.117 --> 00:03:22.952
On the one hand, protect workers' rights; on the other, manage borders fairly. A balanced approach avoids both open-door slogans and blanket bans.

53
00:03:24.100 --> 00:03:25.531
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:26.383 --> 00:03:30.159
Grammar focus: Balanced argument structures (On the one hand).

55
00:03:30.459 --> 00:03:31.716
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:32.417 --> 00:03:38.143
On the one hand, migration fills labour gaps; on the other, it raises integration challenges.

57
00:03:39.050 --> 00:03:44.277
On the one hand, remittances help families; on the other hand, talent leaves poorer countries.

58
00:03:45.183 --> 00:03:48.739
A balanced policy protects rights while managing borders fairly.

59
00:03:49.700 --> 00:04:00.238
On the one hand … on the other (hand) presents two sides. Keep both parts parallel. Use 'however' or 'yet' only for a stronger contrast; use 'on the other hand' to complete the pair.

60
00:04:00.983 --> 00:04:05.818
Be careful! Don't say: On one hand migration helps, other raises.

61
00:04:07.767 --> 00:04:12.239
Say: On the one hand it helps; on the other, it raises costs.

62
00:04:12.539 --> 00:04:14.202
On the one hand… on the other.

63
00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:17.826
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:18.633 --> 00:04:20.029
Two Sides of Movement.

65
00:04:20.329 --> 00:04:23.687
Few topics divide public opinion as sharply as migration.

66
00:04:24.700 --> 00:04:31.402
On the one hand, migrants fill labour gaps, pay taxes and send remittances that sustain families and national economies.

67
00:04:32.400 --> 00:04:38.579
On the other hand, rapid arrivals can strain housing and schools, and poorer countries may lose skilled workers.

68
00:04:39.567 --> 00:04:46.466
Refugees raise distinct obligations, since international law requires states to protect those who flee persecution.

69
00:04:47.467 --> 00:04:55.689
Effective policy therefore balances the protection of rights with the orderly management of borders, rather than choosing one at the expense of the other.

70
00:04:56.800 --> 00:05:00.790
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:01.650 --> 00:05:06.575
On the one hand, migrants fill gaps; blank the other, integration is hard.

72
00:05:06.875 --> 00:05:09.461
A, on. B, in. C, at.

73
00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:15.116
The answer is A: on.

74
00:05:16.250 --> 00:05:18.853
Money sent home by migrant workers is blank.

75
00:05:19.153 --> 00:05:22.691
A, remittances. B, rights. C, labour.

76
00:05:26.233 --> 00:05:28.720
The answer is A: remittances.

77
00:05:29.883 --> 00:05:32.312
A person forced to flee danger is a blank.

78
00:05:32.612 --> 00:05:36.463
A, refugee. B, labourer. C, citizen.

79
00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:42.162
The answer is A: refugee.

80
00:05:43.317 --> 00:05:45.165
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:05:45.465 --> 00:05:56.061
A, On the one hand it helps; on the other it costs.. B, On one hand it helps; on one other it costs.. C, On the one hand it helps; on the other hand costs it..

82
00:05:59.600 --> 00:06:03.759
The answer is A: On the one hand it helps; on the other it costs..

83
00:06:04.917 --> 00:06:09.238
According to the reading, effective policy balances rights protection with…

84
00:06:09.538 --> 00:06:15.467
A, closing all borders. B, orderly border management. C, ignoring refugees.

85
00:06:19.033 --> 00:06:21.741
The answer is B: orderly border management.

86
00:06:22.933 --> 00:06:25.934
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:26.783 --> 00:06:29.491
How do remittances affect families and economies?

88
00:06:46.283 --> 00:06:47.679
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:47.979 --> 00:07:14.045
Remittances have large effects. For families, money from relatives abroad pays for food, schooling and health care. For economies, it provides foreign currency and supports local businesses. On the other hand, dependence on remittances can discourage local job creation, and families may be separated for years. The benefit is real, but so is the social cost.

90
00:07:14.345 --> 00:07:16.089
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:16.983 --> 00:07:22.271
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and balanced argument structures (on the one hand).

92
00:07:22.571 --> 00:07:28.265
Today's value is human dignity. Every person deserves respect wherever they are.

93
00:07:28.565 --> 00:07:31.380
See you in Unit twenty-seven. Goodbye!

94
00:07:31.680 --> 00:07:34.193
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

